WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:02.795
- Your sky and my sky might be different,

00:00:02.795 --> 00:00:03.916
you know what I mean?

00:00:03.916 --> 00:00:05.916
But neither of us will touch the sky

00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:07.227
if we give ourselves boundaries.

00:00:07.227 --> 00:00:10.060
The one thing I really
like to do is drama.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.665
If I aspired to be on the
levels Denzel Washington

00:00:13.665 --> 00:00:15.185
and the Robert De Niros of the world,

00:00:15.185 --> 00:00:17.067
I had to reinvent myself.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:20.567
Whatever I try and do, I
do it from a point of view

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:21.892
as, do I believe in it?

00:00:21.892 --> 00:00:22.725
Yes.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:25.234
I think that, you know, if
you're an acting student,

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:26.939
you know, be a student of the game.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:28.370
Understand the game as much as you can.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.833
Oh, look at that monster!
- Yeah (laughs).

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:32.500
- Do you see it yet?

00:00:33.543 --> 00:00:34.543
Actually, if I can see myself doing that,

00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:35.911
I could probably do it.

00:00:35.911 --> 00:00:38.288
One thing I kept doing
is inspiring myself,

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:40.564
looking for inspiration and growing.

00:00:40.564 --> 00:00:42.447
Oh God, I want to walk into rooms

00:00:42.447 --> 00:00:45.279
where people have been,
lived, seen, smelled,

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:47.137
touched, felt Mr. Mandela.

00:00:47.137 --> 00:00:49.968
There was a drive to be famous,

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:53.173
to be that pinnacle, to be that guy.

00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:56.283
- He's a British actor, musician, and DJ.

00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:58.749
He's best known for his
portrayal of the drug lord

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:01.259
on the HBO series The Wire.

00:01:01.259 --> 00:01:03.566
He's been nominated five
times for a Golden Globe

00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:05.740
for best actor in a miniseries.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.899
He's Idris Elba and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:12.031
(loud explosion)

00:01:12.031 --> 00:01:13.898
I mean, it's important not
to give yourself boundaries,

00:01:13.898 --> 00:01:17.567
which we can do quite easily, okay?

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.922
People talk about aiming for the skies.

00:01:19.922 --> 00:01:23.445
Sometimes even aiming for
the skies is intimidating.

00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:26.807
Just don't give yourself boundaries.

00:01:26.807 --> 00:01:30.162
Your sky and my sky might be different,

00:01:30.162 --> 00:01:31.283
you know what I mean?

00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:33.967
But neither of us will touch
the sky if we give ourselves

00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:35.902
boundaries, you know what I mean?

00:01:35.902 --> 00:01:37.969
Whatever your sky is, whatever
your biggest dream is,

00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:40.652
I might have already
touched that, you know?

00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:42.821
So in other words, what sky?

00:01:42.821 --> 00:01:43.905
Where does the sky end?

00:01:43.905 --> 00:01:45.914
I'd rather people go, "You know what?

00:01:45.914 --> 00:01:48.012
"I'm not going to
restrict myself to going,

00:01:48.012 --> 00:01:50.071
"'I can only do this
or I can only do that,

00:01:50.071 --> 00:01:52.323
"'or I have to do it this way
or I have to do it that way.'"

00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:54.448
I think it's important
that people just go,

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:56.390
"I'm going to lose the boundaries,

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.796
"lose the conformity, and just go for it."

00:01:59.796 --> 00:02:02.578
It was a real sort of liberating feeling

00:02:02.578 --> 00:02:04.245
at 16, 17 years old.

00:02:05.114 --> 00:02:07.934
The one thing I really
liked to do was drama

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:12.227
and there was no sort of
beacon for this could be

00:02:12.227 --> 00:02:13.964
a career, you could do it.

00:02:13.964 --> 00:02:17.153
I just didn't see that, but
I really loved to do it,

00:02:17.153 --> 00:02:20.444
so I just said, "I want
to do what I love,"

00:02:20.444 --> 00:02:21.463
you know what I mean?
- So it was like,

00:02:21.463 --> 00:02:23.966
school plays or did you?
- Yeah, school plays

00:02:23.966 --> 00:02:27.704
and from school, you know, literally,

00:02:27.704 --> 00:02:29.130
my fear.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.903
My drama teacher was like,
"You got some talent here,

00:02:30.903 --> 00:02:32.881
"brother, you know?

00:02:32.881 --> 00:02:34.345
"You did really well on your GCSEs.

00:02:34.345 --> 00:02:35.871
"You got some talent.

00:02:35.871 --> 00:02:38.157
"Why don't you do it?"

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.739
And she gave me this course.

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:42.392
There's a two-year performing arts course.

00:02:42.392 --> 00:02:43.386
It was a new course.

00:02:43.386 --> 00:02:45.500
And she's like, "Do the
course, fall on your face

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.788
"for two years, paint scenery,

00:02:47.788 --> 00:02:50.488
"direct plays, just do it for two years

00:02:50.488 --> 00:02:51.552
"and see if you like it."

00:02:51.552 --> 00:02:52.531
And that's what I did.

00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:53.736
That's when I fell in love with it.

00:02:53.736 --> 00:02:56.889
So I got to a certain point in my career

00:02:56.889 --> 00:02:58.904
where I saw the glass ceiling.

00:02:58.904 --> 00:03:01.694
I was so close to it, I was
going to hit my forehead on it,

00:03:01.694 --> 00:03:03.446
but I was busy.

00:03:03.446 --> 00:03:04.390
I was busy.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.595
I got lots of work.

00:03:05.595 --> 00:03:08.476
I realized that I could only
play so many best friends

00:03:08.476 --> 00:03:10.546
or gang leaders, all right?

00:03:10.546 --> 00:03:13.655
I knew that I wasn't going
to land a leading role.

00:03:13.655 --> 00:03:16.272
I knew there wasn't enough imagination,

00:03:16.272 --> 00:03:20.265
not yet, for the industry
to be seeing me as a lead.

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:23.567
In other words, if I wanted
to star in a British drama

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.243
like Luther, for example,

00:03:25.243 --> 00:03:28.559
then I'd have to go to a
country like America, all right?

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:30.502
Now some people might say back then,

00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:32.765
"Look, there was no black detectives?

00:03:32.765 --> 00:03:34.555
"You know, like, how can you expect us

00:03:34.555 --> 00:03:37.651
"to make a TV show about one?"

00:03:37.651 --> 00:03:39.144
It's television!

00:03:39.144 --> 00:03:40.713
(audience laughs)
Sorry.

00:03:40.713 --> 00:03:43.061
And the other thing is
that I didn't see myself

00:03:43.061 --> 00:03:45.775
or my culture on TV, so I literally

00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:47.186
just stopped watching it.

00:03:47.186 --> 00:03:50.436
Instead, I decided to go and become TV.

00:03:51.271 --> 00:03:53.708
Now if I aspired to be on the levels

00:03:53.708 --> 00:03:57.134
of Denzel Washingtons and the
Robert De Niros in the world,

00:03:57.134 --> 00:03:58.784
I had to reinvent myself.

00:03:58.784 --> 00:04:01.891
I had to transform the
way the industry saw me.

00:04:01.891 --> 00:04:04.438
I had to climb out of that box.

00:04:04.438 --> 00:04:06.518
In other words, I didn't go to America

00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:08.255
because I couldn't get parts.

00:04:08.255 --> 00:04:10.885
I went to America because
I was running out of parts.

00:04:10.885 --> 00:04:13.628
All the parts were the same.

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:16.936
Whatever I try and do, I do
it from a point of view as,

00:04:16.936 --> 00:04:18.352
do I believe in it?

00:04:18.352 --> 00:04:19.490
Yes.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:20.910
I don't do things for money

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.395
and I've been broke most of my life.

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:25.369
So because of that,

00:04:25.369 --> 00:04:27.274
I can't sort of suddenly go,

00:04:27.274 --> 00:04:29.312
"I need to do a job
just to get some cash,"

00:04:29.312 --> 00:04:30.200
you know what I mean?

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.527
I know that's, a lot of times,

00:04:31.527 --> 00:04:33.867
it is people's motivations
because people do need to eat,

00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:35.416
but in my position,

00:04:35.416 --> 00:04:37.459
I've been poor most of my life,

00:04:37.459 --> 00:04:39.715
so now, I don't really
do things based on money.

00:04:39.715 --> 00:04:42.448
I do things based on
authenticity of how I feel.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.661
I think that, you know, if
you're an acting student,

00:04:44.661 --> 00:04:46.228
you know, be a student of the game,

00:04:46.228 --> 00:04:47.839
understand the game as much as you can.

00:04:47.839 --> 00:04:49.954
It's very important to know the territory

00:04:49.954 --> 00:04:51.725
that you want to be in.

00:04:51.725 --> 00:04:53.764
If it's a film director, or an actor,

00:04:53.764 --> 00:04:56.609
or whatever that is, really understand

00:04:56.609 --> 00:05:00.270
what you're trying to
achieve and understand

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.182
the marketplace, if you like.

00:05:03.182 --> 00:05:05.295
There's nothing worse than an actor

00:05:05.295 --> 00:05:07.780
that has grandiose ideas of being famous

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.880
but doesn't actually
understand the root it takes

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.085
to get from one place
to another as an actor.

00:05:14.085 --> 00:05:16.708
So that's about self-education, of course

00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:20.823
and trying to put
yourself in opportunities

00:05:20.823 --> 00:05:24.240
where you can always practice your craft,

00:05:25.141 --> 00:05:27.891
whether that's doing small plays,

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:31.682
whether that's writing
scripts for yourself,

00:05:31.682 --> 00:05:35.765
whether that's audition
and doing open auditions.

00:05:36.955 --> 00:05:38.312
As a young actor,

00:05:38.312 --> 00:05:40.580
try as much as you can to
be around the community

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.940
of filmmakers and theater

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.022
so you can soak it all up like a sponge.

00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:47.915
- I feel like coming off
a movie like Pacific Rim

00:05:47.915 --> 00:05:49.913
where you're using language
and you're talking about

00:05:49.913 --> 00:05:51.676
things that aren't real,

00:05:51.676 --> 00:05:53.344
you're doing that, and
I'm sure some of the stuff

00:05:53.344 --> 00:05:54.182
you're talking about you don't even know

00:05:54.182 --> 00:05:55.318
what you're talking about, right?

00:05:55.318 --> 00:05:56.280
(Idris laughs)

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.970
To go from that to
something that is so real

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.111
- Yeah.
- What's the transition

00:06:00.111 --> 00:06:00.991
like in your head?

00:06:00.991 --> 00:06:02.200
- [Idris] I mean, it was nuts actually

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.513
because I was here shooting that film,

00:06:04.513 --> 00:06:07.006
going off and talking about,
"Oh, look at that monster!"

00:06:07.006 --> 00:06:08.560
- Yeah (laughs).

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.227
- Do you see it yet?

00:06:11.117 --> 00:06:13.710
And then reading about Mr. Mandela

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.010
and having to prepare for that.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.426
That was a massive contrast for me

00:06:16.426 --> 00:06:18.093
and I think it is really,

00:06:18.093 --> 00:06:19.245
it's, you know,

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:21.376
if a guitarist picks up a guitar

00:06:21.376 --> 00:06:23.226
and he can play a reggae
song and a rock song

00:06:23.226 --> 00:06:25.295
using the one instrument,
that's the beauty

00:06:25.295 --> 00:06:26.646
of the instrument and that's, I think,

00:06:26.646 --> 00:06:29.031
the beauty of an actor,
is being able to do that

00:06:29.031 --> 00:06:30.728
- Right.
- And have that sort of span,

00:06:30.728 --> 00:06:32.003
you know?

00:06:32.003 --> 00:06:35.476
I think my imagination's
always kept me going,

00:06:35.476 --> 00:06:37.025
you know what I mean?

00:06:37.025 --> 00:06:40.137
It's like, just imagine myself

00:06:40.137 --> 00:06:41.974
collecting awards.

00:06:41.974 --> 00:06:44.363
Just imagine myself getting big parts.

00:06:44.363 --> 00:06:46.636
Imagine myself working
with the greatest actors.

00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:48.476
My imagination was the one like,

00:06:48.476 --> 00:06:49.613
you know what I mean?

00:06:49.613 --> 00:06:51.472
Not overthink it, but
you're just kind of like,

00:06:51.472 --> 00:06:54.363
"Yeah man, I see myself
driving that car one day,"

00:06:54.363 --> 00:06:56.285
and I get that, you know what I mean?

00:06:56.285 --> 00:06:59.597
That's part of my "inner magic,"

00:06:59.597 --> 00:07:02.262
is that actually, if I
can see myself doing that,

00:07:02.262 --> 00:07:03.523
I could probably do it.

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.804
So you can't bottle it up
and sell it to somebody.

00:07:05.804 --> 00:07:07.465
You either can do it or you can't,

00:07:07.465 --> 00:07:09.348
but everybody can imagine themselves,

00:07:09.348 --> 00:07:10.619
you know what I'm saying?

00:07:10.619 --> 00:07:12.050
Yeah, you know what?

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.047
Actually, yeah.

00:07:13.047 --> 00:07:15.585
I can see myself being
an influencer in clothes,

00:07:15.585 --> 00:07:17.359
you know what I mean?

00:07:17.359 --> 00:07:18.412
Because I can make that happen.

00:07:18.412 --> 00:07:21.829
Yes, you know my history of being sort of

00:07:22.848 --> 00:07:25.098
on all sides of my journey.

00:07:26.252 --> 00:07:28.448
I didn't have success very early

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:31.184
and I went through some very dark times

00:07:31.184 --> 00:07:32.852
and do you know what I mean?

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:35.505
One thing I kept doing
is inspiring myself,

00:07:35.505 --> 00:07:37.538
looking for inspiration and growing,

00:07:37.538 --> 00:07:40.690
and it hasn't stopped and
I want to keep doing that.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:41.523
And I also

00:07:42.357 --> 00:07:44.412
want to inspire others, you know?

00:07:44.412 --> 00:07:47.136
There are millions of me
sitting at home right now

00:07:47.136 --> 00:07:49.223
and they don't know it

00:07:49.223 --> 00:07:50.866
and it's just good to be able to get

00:07:50.866 --> 00:07:52.636
this sort of platform to sort of say,

00:07:52.636 --> 00:07:53.469
"Come on, man.

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:54.571
"Go for it."

00:07:54.571 --> 00:07:56.113
There was a lot of encouragement to trust

00:07:56.113 --> 00:07:57.554
my instincts, you know?

00:07:57.554 --> 00:08:01.275
The instinct of use your presence,
you know what I'm saying?

00:08:01.275 --> 00:08:05.030
And like, in South Africa, you know.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.473
Obviously, I'm not South African,

00:08:06.473 --> 00:08:09.158
so I'm really aware that
oh God, I'm going to walk

00:08:09.158 --> 00:08:11.592
into rooms where people have been, lived,

00:08:11.592 --> 00:08:13.885
seen, smelled, touched, felt Mr. Mandela

00:08:13.885 --> 00:08:16.087
in person, on the radio, on television.

00:08:16.087 --> 00:08:19.925
And here I come as an outsider doing this

00:08:19.925 --> 00:08:21.381
portrayal of him.

00:08:21.381 --> 00:08:22.723
I was really aware.

00:08:22.723 --> 00:08:23.698
It used to make me really nervous about

00:08:23.698 --> 00:08:26.531
if I'm going to do it right or not

00:08:27.739 --> 00:08:28.587
and Justin

00:08:28.587 --> 00:08:30.935
encouraged me to stay

00:08:30.935 --> 00:08:32.685
with what my gut was.

00:08:33.728 --> 00:08:35.562
Just stay with that.

00:08:35.562 --> 00:08:38.001
Don't waver from it, don't
try to be their Mandela

00:08:38.001 --> 00:08:40.831
or the Mandela they think you should be.

00:08:40.831 --> 00:08:43.306
Be that guy we've discussed
in all the rehearsals

00:08:43.306 --> 00:08:44.876
and all the script meetings, you know?

00:08:44.876 --> 00:08:47.098
Be that guy, go with that thing.

00:08:47.098 --> 00:08:48.723
So that was great, actually.

00:08:48.723 --> 00:08:50.214
I took Justin's credit.

00:08:50.214 --> 00:08:53.597
Like, he really helped
me sculpt and measure out

00:08:53.597 --> 00:08:55.964
that performance, you know?

00:08:55.964 --> 00:08:57.501
I was 16

00:08:57.501 --> 00:08:59.519
contemplating being an actor.

00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:02.873
I was in Sound Systems
DJing, you know what I mean?

00:09:02.873 --> 00:09:04.728
And it was like, that or this?

00:09:04.728 --> 00:09:06.184
So I always go back to DJing.

00:09:06.184 --> 00:09:08.525
I always have a studio, always
have turntables, always.

00:09:08.525 --> 00:09:10.256
- [Interviewer] Your feet firmly fell

00:09:10.256 --> 00:09:11.257
planted on the ground?

00:09:11.257 --> 00:09:12.218
- Are they?

00:09:12.218 --> 00:09:13.051
I don't know.

00:09:13.051 --> 00:09:13.884
- [Interviewer] Well, I don't know.

00:09:13.884 --> 00:09:14.717
Actors, it's hard.

00:09:14.717 --> 00:09:16.145
- It is and.
- It's hard to get

00:09:16.145 --> 00:09:17.213
all this attention and stay sane.

00:09:17.213 --> 00:09:18.046
- Yeah.

00:09:18.902 --> 00:09:20.359
I think, you know what I mean?

00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:21.688
Like for me, listen.

00:09:21.688 --> 00:09:23.155
I've been doing it a long time.

00:09:23.155 --> 00:09:24.436
You know what I mean?

00:09:24.436 --> 00:09:26.655
And there was a drive

00:09:26.655 --> 00:09:28.268
to be famous,

00:09:28.268 --> 00:09:30.797
to be that pinnacle, to be that guy

00:09:30.797 --> 00:09:32.306
and I'm not.

00:09:32.306 --> 00:09:35.049
My ambition isn't that as much,

00:09:35.049 --> 00:09:36.327
you know what I mean?

00:09:36.327 --> 00:09:37.910
Good work, keep working.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.485
So it would be hard
for me to sort of like,

00:09:39.485 --> 00:09:41.223
suddenly be like, "Oh, I'm lifted up

00:09:41.223 --> 00:09:43.511
"by this wonderful life that I have."

00:09:43.511 --> 00:09:44.598
I love it, but you know,

00:09:44.598 --> 00:09:45.854
my feet are on the ground, you know?

00:09:45.854 --> 00:09:47.544
It's, you know, it's work.

00:09:47.544 --> 00:09:48.719
It's a job.

00:09:48.719 --> 00:09:51.487
(loud whooshing air)

00:09:51.487 --> 00:09:53.174
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:53.174 --> 00:09:56.237
I made this video because
Canif Ageric asked me to,

00:09:56.237 --> 00:09:57.237
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:09:57.237 --> 00:09:58.548
that you want me to profile next,

00:09:58.548 --> 00:09:59.918
leave it in the comments below

00:09:59.918 --> 00:10:00.953
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:00.953 --> 00:10:03.718
I'd also love to know which
of Elba's top 10 rules

00:10:03.718 --> 00:10:05.187
meant the most to you,

00:10:05.187 --> 00:10:07.280
is going to have the
biggest impact on your life.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.570
Leave it in the comments.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:09.528
I'll join the discussion.

00:10:09.528 --> 00:10:10.730
Thank you so much for watching.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:14.417
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:10:14.417 --> 00:10:17.167
(loud explosion)

00:10:19.722 --> 00:10:22.704
- I do think that, you
know, it is a balancing act

00:10:22.704 --> 00:10:27.272
and the truth is, it's
about forward planning,

00:10:27.272 --> 00:10:31.328
having a good team around
you, you know what I mean?

00:10:31.328 --> 00:10:34.314
To help me and help me see perspective

00:10:34.314 --> 00:10:36.491
because one thing about being in the front

00:10:36.491 --> 00:10:38.145
is that you can't see what's behind you,

00:10:38.145 --> 00:10:40.074
but if you've got people
that are around you

00:10:40.074 --> 00:10:41.437
to give you a little bit of vision,

00:10:41.437 --> 00:10:42.462
you can go, "All right, okay.

00:10:42.462 --> 00:10:44.238
"Actually, this will work
and this won't work,"

00:10:44.238 --> 00:10:45.419
you know what I mean?

00:10:45.419 --> 00:10:47.383
But when you're just
looking forward sometimes,

00:10:47.383 --> 00:10:48.359
you won't see that.

00:10:48.359 --> 00:10:49.998
- [Interviewer] When you
realized it wasn't a joke,

00:10:49.998 --> 00:10:52.835
was your first instinct stay away from it?

00:10:52.835 --> 00:10:55.958
Or was it percolating in the
back of your head somewhere

00:10:55.958 --> 00:10:58.070
as a great idea?

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.295
- It was a couple of things happening

00:11:00.295 --> 00:11:01.790
and my first instinct was, "Dude, dude.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:04.135
"That is the opportunity of a lifetime.

00:11:04.135 --> 00:11:05.261
"Are you kidding me?

00:11:05.261 --> 00:11:06.446
"What a gift."

00:11:06.446 --> 00:11:08.436
But then, it was the next thought was,

00:11:08.436 --> 00:11:09.724
"But if you fuck it up, goodnight.

00:11:09.724 --> 00:11:11.022
"Your career is over,

00:11:11.022 --> 00:11:12.531
"period."

00:11:12.531 --> 00:11:14.046
You know?

00:11:14.046 --> 00:11:16.646
Because in film, you know, my career

00:11:16.646 --> 00:11:18.736
has been decent and I
can't say I have a string

00:11:18.736 --> 00:11:21.548
of amazing films under my belt, you know?

00:11:21.548 --> 00:11:23.895
I think I've got great films and films

00:11:23.895 --> 00:11:27.168
that vary from popcorn films
to some really heartfelt films,

00:11:27.168 --> 00:11:29.395
but you know, Mandela
was the next level for me

00:11:29.395 --> 00:11:33.477
and it was absolutely the
gateway to the next chapter

00:11:33.477 --> 00:11:34.714
of my career.

00:11:34.714 --> 00:11:37.544
But had I had to the
gateway and tripped up

00:11:37.544 --> 00:11:39.143
as I fall in, I wouldn't,

00:11:39.143 --> 00:11:42.381
I won't be moving on
because he's such a big,

00:11:42.381 --> 00:11:44.214
world, global citizen,

00:11:45.246 --> 00:11:46.079
you know?

00:11:46.079 --> 00:11:48.013
And I had all this sort of trepidation

00:11:48.013 --> 00:11:50.232
about you know, "Shit man, I
don't look like Mr. Mandela

00:11:50.232 --> 00:11:51.307
"at all.

00:11:51.307 --> 00:11:52.515
"How in the hell am I going to do this?"

00:11:52.515 --> 00:11:54.962
But Justin was really
clear about his vision

00:11:54.962 --> 00:11:56.952
and then it was a different spin, I guess.

00:11:56.952 --> 00:11:58.582
- What is our theory in success?

00:11:58.582 --> 00:12:00.595
what is your mantra around this?

00:12:00.595 --> 00:12:02.101
- You know, it's a good question.

00:12:02.101 --> 00:12:05.407
I don't know if I've actually
ever thought about success

00:12:05.407 --> 00:12:06.407
in that way.

00:12:08.614 --> 00:12:12.144
Obviously, I feel a successful actor,

00:12:12.144 --> 00:12:14.917
but I also felt successful the first day

00:12:14.917 --> 00:12:16.539
I got my first job.

00:12:16.539 --> 00:12:18.391
You know, that was a form of success.

00:12:18.391 --> 00:12:19.974
I think ultimately,

00:12:21.422 --> 00:12:23.279
if you're successful in whatever you do,

00:12:23.279 --> 00:12:25.073
it's about maintenance, isn't it?

00:12:25.073 --> 00:12:27.400
That, I think, is the most important thing

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:28.654
is maintaining success.

00:12:28.654 --> 00:12:30.503
You know, you get to the pinnacle,

00:12:30.503 --> 00:12:31.800
but how do you stay up there?

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:32.810
And that, I think, is important.

